%% Answer plans for every past T3 question — RN volume only.

## Every past question as an answer plan

Use these plans in the last fortnight: read the question, say the plan aloud from memory, then check. Each plan is the skeleton of the full model answer in T3QA.

**2016 Q5 — Women's movement in Pakistan; strengths and weaknesses.** Define; Mumtaz and Shaheed (1987); the movement as a network (APWA, WAF, Sindhiani Tehreek, LHWs, religious women, lawyers, legislators, digital activists). Roots (Mumtaz Ali 1898; League women 1938; Quaid 1944). Phases: welfare (APWA 1949; Bogra 1955 → MFLO 1961); resistance (Hudood 1979; WAF 1981; 12 Feb 1983); reform (Benazir 1988; Beijing 1995; CEDAW 1996; NCSW 2000; 60 seats 2002; 2006, 2010, 2011, 2016 laws); new activism (Aurat March 2018; online outrage after Qandeel 2016 and motorway 2020; 2021 and 2025 laws). Timeline figure. Strengths (Islamic and constitutional legitimacy; Asma Jahangir and Hina Jilani, AGHS; resilience; caucus 2008; adaptability; research base). Weaknesses (elite base; enforcement; dependence and NGO-isation; backlash; divisions). Compare figure. Scorecard (143/145; 24.4%; 11 women). Way forward. Two steps forward.

**2018 Q8(b) — Short note: APWA and WAF.** (~540 words.) APWA: 1949; Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan founder and first president; from Partition relief; welfare — schools, colleges, clinics, crafts; UN consultative status; protests over Bogra's marriage → Rashid Commission → MFLO 1961. WAF: Karachi 1981; Fehmida–Allah Bux; non-hierarchical, non-party, secular, unfunded; Hudood, Law of Evidence; 12 Feb 1983; Safia Bibi. Comparison table (era, aim, method, politics, achievement, criticism). Legacy: institutions vs rights movement; shared urban base; two stages of one movement; Aurat March inherits both.

**2019 Q3 / 2023 Q5 — Three waves in the West; influence on Pakistan.** Lear 1968. First wave: coverture; Stanton and Mott, London 1840 → Seneca Falls 1848; NUWSS 1897 / WSPU 1903; NZ 1893, UK 1918/28, US 1920; limits. In Pakistan: vote with independence; APWA; 1948 inheritance Act; MFLO 1961. Second wave: Friedan 1963, NOW 1966, personal is political, Equal Pay Act, Title VII, Roe 1973, shelters 1971; hooks' critique. In Pakistan: WAF vs Hudood (1979/1981); 12 Feb 1983; adaptation (Constitution, Islam; scholars invited); legacy 2006, 2010, 2016. Third wave: Anita Hill 1991; Walker 1992; intersectionality, Butler, Mohanty; Beijing. In Pakistan: Beijing (Benazir), CEDAW 1996, NGOs, diversity, *khawaja siras*, Islamic feminism. Mapping figure. Fourth wave: #MeToo; Aurat March. Influence vs roots (tools and platforms vs local roots; Women's Division 1979, NCSW 2000). Waves overlap. **2023 refresh:** add 2021/2025 laws, fourth wave, newest data, sharper verdict.

**2022 Q4 — First wave and third wave.** Equal treatment under the same sub-headings. First: context (coverture; closed universities; custody); Seneca Falls and the Declaration; NWSA 1869; 19th Amendment; suffragists vs suffragettes; goals, methods, liberal theory (Mill 1869); achievements (NZ 1893, Finland 1906, UK, US; property Acts); limits (race, class; family untouched). Third: context (second-wave gains; Anita Hill; postfeminism); Walker 1992; Crenshaw, hooks, Butler, Mohanty; Riot Grrrl, Wolf, Ensler; goals and methods; VAWA 1994; limits (choice feminism, girl power). Compare figure; shared core. Pakistan: vote 1947, APWA, 1948; Beijing, CEDAW, Islamic feminism. Citizenship vs plurality.

**2022 Q6 — Feminist movements in Pakistan in detail.** Plural "movements". Roots. Welfare 1947–70 (APWA; 1948 inheritance; Bogra → MFLO 1961). 1973 Constitution (Arts 25, 27, 34; Ra'ana Governor 1973; promises from above). Zia (Hudood 1979; Qanun-e-Shahadat 1984; WAF 1981; 12 Feb 1983; Safia Bibi). 1988–99 (Benazir; First Women Bank; police stations; LHWs 1994; Beijing; CEDAW; NGOs; HRCP 1987; Samia Sarwar 1999; Hudood not repealed). 2000–17 (NCSW; 33% local; 60 seats; caucus 2008; 2006 Act; 2010, 2011, 2016; Qandeel; state feminism). Pyramid figure. 2018– (Aurat March; motorway 2020 → 2021 Act; ICT 2025). Other movements (Sindhiani Tehreek; LHWs SC 2012; Sindh HBW Act 2018; JI women, Al-Huda 1994; WDF 2018; Kishwar Naheed, Fahmida Riaz, Tehrik-e-Niswan 1979; Asma Jahangir, Hina Jilani). Evaluation; scorecard. Many movements, one struggle.

**2024 Q4 — Impact of Western movements; past vs emerging trends and effects.** Channels (education, UN, donors, media, diaspora). Wave-by-wave impact with adaptation: first (APWA, vote 1947, MFLO in Islamic terms, 1973 Constitution); second (WAF; personal is political; 2006 Act; methods — resource centres, legal aid, Safia Bibi); third (Beijing, CEDAW, NGOs, Islamic feminism, mainstreaming → NPA 1998, NCSW 2000, seats 2002); fourth (#MeToo, Aurat March, DRF, PECA 2016). Limits (local roots; waves together; hostile state; postcolonial critique; local issues). Past (elite; APWA, WAF, NGOs; welfare and law; courts; Constitution and Islam). Emerging (young; collectives, WDF; body, violence, care; marches and social media; consent; independent). Compare figure. Effects: visibility; inclusion; faster laws (2020 → 2021; Noor Mukadam); backlash; digital divide (25%). Past legitimacy + present energy.

**2025 Q4 — Principles, goals and strategies of the waves; effects on women's lives.** Four waves under three fixed sub-headings. First: rational citizens (Wollstonecraft, Mill); vote, property, education, custody; Seneca Falls, petitions, militancy, processions. Second: personal is political; pay, reproduction, violence, housework; NOW lobbying, consciousness-raising, shelters, Women's Studies, books and *Ms.* Third: diversity, intersectionality, construction, choice; inclusion, sexuality, harassment, human rights; culture, zines, Beijing networks, early internet. Fourth: consent, autonomy, accountability; end harassment; hashtags, testimony, Women's March 2017. Pyramid figure. Effects: political and legal (vote; a quarter of MPs; coverture ended; CEDAW); education, work, health (universities; pay laws; gaps; second shift; pill; Roe and Dobbs); safety and voice (violence criminalised; marital rape; #MeToo; diverse voices). Pakistan (1947; laws 1961–2025; Aurat March; waves overlap; 143/145; 24.4%). Unfinished (69.2%, 120 years; one in three; Afghanistan; Beijing+30 backlash). One long movement.

**2026 Q3 — From WAF against Hudood to digital activism; legal rights to bodily autonomy.** Define locus of resistance, legal rights, bodily autonomy. 1980s: Zina Ordinance; WAF 1981 (Fehmida–Allah Bux); 12 Feb 1983; methods (legal analysis, Safia Bibi, Asma Jahangir, Hina Jilani; Constitution and Islam; few public challenges under martial law). 1990s: Benazir; Beijing; CEDAW; NGOs; Samia Sarwar. 2000s–10s: state feminism; NCSW, seats, caucus; 2006 Act (WAF's central demand); 2010, 2011, 2016 — won on paper. 2020s: Aurat March; *Mera Jism Meri Marzi*; #MeToo 2018; Qandeel, motorway, Noor Mukadam → 2021 Act. Flow figure. What shifted (target, issues, language). Why (laws won, violence continued; new generation took gains for granted; fourth wave; body as site of control — izzat). What stayed (march demands laws — ICT 2025; WAF defended bodies). Costs (backlash; lost allies; digital divide 25%). From rights on paper to rights over one's own body.

**2026 Q6 — NGO-isation; depoliticisation; the WAD critique.** Define NGO-isation (Alvarez 1999; Jad 2004), depoliticisation (technocratic project vs social revolution), gender mainstreaming (Beijing 1995), WAD vs WID. How it happened (Shirkat Gah 1975; WAF 1981 unfunded; military rule; post-Beijing donors; service delivery; regulation from 2015). WAD applied (micro-credit and crafts leave land, feudal power, wages, unpaid care; the embroiderer example). Cycle figure. Evidence (donor agendas; projectisation; professionalisation and consultants; upward accountability; urban/class bias; "Western agenda"; competition; NGOs as employers). Mainstreaming as technocracy (NPA 1998; National Policy 2002; GRAPs; checklists fade with funding). Counter-case (NGOs drafted the 2006–2016 laws; kept space; trained councillors 2001; built data; services change power — personal is political; WAF unfunded; Aurat March self-funded; Sindhiani Tehreek). Compare figure. Verdict: partly true, but incomplete; division of labour; local funding; accountability to women.
